<article xml:id="week5" label="0005"> <!-- Change label to a random string of numbers for each worksheet. The label shows up in the url of the website and keeps students from searching for other worksheets -->
  <title>MATH 140 Hands-On, Grades Up</title>
  <introduction>
    <p>
     For workspace between exercises, print this worksheet or download to your device by clicking the printer icon.
    </p>
  </introduction>
<!--START TYPING CONTENT FOR SESSION BELOW HERE-->
  <worksheet>
    <title> Session #5 (Sections 3.4 &amp; 4.1)</title>
    <p>
      Directions: Answer each of the following as best you can. Feel free to discuss your thoughts about the questions with some peers around you.
      The Math 140 textbook is free, and can be viewed at <url href="https://oaktrust.library.tamu.edu/items/7d9f5efe-b63b-4c57-8db3-91790deed00d" visual="oaktrust.library.tamu.edu/items/7d9f5efe-b63b-4c57-8db3-91790deed00d">Texas A&amp;M OAKTrust Digital Repository</url>.
    </p>
    <paragraphs> <!--   %%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%   SECTION 2  %%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%    -->
      <title>Getting Warmed Up and in the Right Mindset</title>
      <exercise><!-- #1 -->
        <introduction>
          <p>
            Use the linear programming problem below to answer the questions that follow.
              <md>
                <mrow>\text{Maximize } \amp P = x+3y+2z</mrow>
                <mrow>\text{subject to } \amp \dfrac{2}{7}x+\dfrac{1}{5}y+\dfrac{3}{4}z\leq 630</mrow>
                <mrow>\amp \dfrac{1}{4}x+\dfrac{1}{8}y+\dfrac{1}{2}z\leq 250</mrow>
                <mrow>\amp x-y+2z\leq 480</mrow>
                <mrow>\amp x\geq 0, y\geq 0, z\geq 0</mrow>
              </md>
          </p>
        </introduction>
        <task>
          <statement>
            <p>
              Complete the simplex tableau by filling in the blanks with the correct element.
              <me>
                \begin{array}{rrrrrrr|r}
                  x \amp y \amp z \amp s_1 \amp s_2 \amp s_3 \amp P \amp c \\
                  \hline
                  \dfrac{2}{7} \amp \_ \amp \_ \amp \_ \amp \_ \amp \_ \amp 0 \amp 630 \\
                  \_ \amp \_ \amp \dfrac{1}{2} \amp \_ \amp \_ \amp \_ \amp 0 \amp 250 \\
                  \_ \amp -1 \amp \_ \amp \_ \amp \_ \amp \_ \amp 0 \amp 480 \\
                  \hline
                  \_ \amp \_ \amp \_ \amp 0 \amp 0 \amp 0 \amp 1 \amp 0
                \end{array}
              </me>
            </p>
          </statement>
          <answer>
            <p>
              <me>
                \begin{array}{rrrrrrr|r}
                  x \amp y \amp z \amp s_1 \amp s_2 \amp s_3 \amp P \amp c \\
                  \hline
                  \dfrac{2}{7} \amp \dfrac{1}{5} \amp \dfrac{3}{4} \amp 1 \amp 0 \amp 0 \amp 0 \amp 630 \\
                  \dfrac{1}{4} \amp \dfrac{1}{8} \amp \dfrac{1}{2} \amp 0 \amp 1 \amp 0 \amp 0 \amp 250 \\
                  1 \amp -1 \amp 2 \amp 0 \amp 0 \amp 1 \amp 0 \amp 480 \\
                  \hline
                  -1 \amp -3 \amp -2 \amp 0 \amp 0 \amp 0 \amp 1 \amp 0
                \end{array}
              </me>
            </p>
          </answer>
        </task>
        <task>
          <statement>
            <p>
              For the simplex tableau above, the pivot column is in which column?
            </p>
          </statement>
          <hint>
            <p>
              The pivot column is identified by looking for the most negative entry in the bottom row (i.e., the row representing the objective function). 
            </p>
          </hint>
          <answer>
            <p>
              Column <m>2</m>
            </p>
          </answer>
        </task>
        <task>
          <statement>
            <p>
              Compute the test ratios for each row, except the last row.
            </p>
          </statement>
          <hint>
            <p>
              The ratios are computed by dividing the far-right column (denoted with a <m>c</m>) by the corresponding value in the pivot column of that row. A ratio that has a zero or a negative number in the denominator is ignored.
            </p>
          </hint>
          <answer>
            <p>
              Row <m>1</m>: <m>\dfrac{630}{\frac{1}{5}}=3150</m>, Row <m>2</m>: <m>\dfrac{250}{\frac{1}{8}}=2000</m>, Row <m>3</m>: <m>\dfrac{480}{-1}=-480</m>
            </p>
          </answer>
        </task>
        <task>
          <statement>
            <p>
              Given the information above, which row is the pivot row?
            </p>
          </statement>
          <hint>
            <p>
              The smallest non-negative ratio computed identifies the pivot row.
            </p>
          </hint>
          <answer>
            <p>
              Row <m>2</m>
            </p>
          </answer>
        </task>
        <task>
          <statement>
            <p>
              Identify the pivot element in the original simplex tableau.
            </p>
          </statement>
          <answer>
            <p>
              <m>\dfrac{1}{8}</m>, Row <m>2</m> Column <m>2</m>
            </p>
          </answer>
        </task>
      </exercise>
      <exercise><!-- #2 -->
          <statement>
            <p>
              Fill in each blank with the correct words. A list of possible words is given in the word bank below.
          <sidebyside widths="28% 22% 22% 28%" valign="middle">
            <p>
              <ul>
                <li>
                  <p>
                    random experiment
                  </p>
                </li>
                <li>
                  <p>
                    <m>2^n</m>
                  </p>
                </li>
                <li>
                  <p>
                    certain
                  </p>
                </li>
                <li>
                  <p>
                    complement
                  </p>
                </li>
              </ul>
            </p>
            <p>
              <ul>
                <li>
                  <p>
                    simple
                  </p>
                </li>
                <li>
                  <p>
                    subset
                  </p>
                </li>
                <li>
                  <p>
                    outcomes
                  </p>
                </li>
                <li>
                  <p>
                    both <m>E</m> and <m>F</m>
                  </p>
                </li>
              </ul>
            </p>
            <p>
              <ul>
                <li>
                  <p>
                    sample space
                  </p>
                </li>
                <li>
                  <p>
                    impossible
                  </p>
                </li>
                <li>
                  <p>
                    <m>\emptyset</m>
                  </p>
                </li>
                <li>
                  <p>
                    <m>E \cap F</m>
                  </p>
                </li>
              </ul>
            </p>
            <p>
              <ul>
                <li>
                  <p>
                    both
                  </p>
                </li>
                <li>
                  <p>
                    <m>E \cup F</m>
                  </p>
                </li>
                <li>
                  <p>
                    union
                  </p>
                </li>
                <li>
                  <p>
                    mutually exclusive
                  </p>
                </li>
              </ul>
            </p>

          </sidebyside>
              <ol>
                <li>
                  <p>
                    A(n) _____ is an activity or observation whose results cannot be predicted ahead of time.
                  </p>
                </li>
                <li>
                  <p>
                    _____ are the results of a random experiment.
                  </p>
                </li>
                <li>
                  <p>
                    The _____, <m>S</m>, is the set of all possible outcomes for a random experiment.
                  </p>
                </li>
                <li>
                  <p>
                    An event, <m>E</m>, is a _____ of the sample space (a collection of outcomes from an experiment).
                  </p>
                </li>
                <li>
                  <p>
                    A(n) _____ event is an event with exactly one outcome.
                  </p>
                </li>
                <li>
                  <p>
                    The _____ event, often denoted by <m>S</m>, is the event containing all outcomes of an experiment (the entire sample space).
                  </p>
                </li>
                <li>
                  <p>
                    A(n) _____ event, often denoted as <m>\{\}</m> or _____, is an event containing no outcomes.
                  </p>
                </li>
                <li>
                  <p>
                    In general, for a sample space with <m>n</m> outcomes, there are <m>n</m> simple events, and the total number of possible events is _____.
                  </p>
                </li>
                <li>
                  <p>
                    The _____ of event <m>A</m>, denoted by <m>A^c</m>, is the event that contains all outcomes in the sample space which are NOT in event <m>A</m>.
                  </p>
                </li>
                <li>
                  <p>
                    The intersection of events <m>E</m> and <m>F</m>, denoted _____, is the event that contains all outcomes in the sample space which _____ have in common.
                  </p>
                </li>
                <li>
                  <p>
                    If two events, <m>E</m> and <m>F</m>, have no outcomes in common, then their intersection is impossible, <m>E \cap F = \emptyset</m>; the events <m>E</m> and <m>F</m> are called _____, or disjoint, events.
                  </p>
                </li>
                <li>
                  <p>
                    The _____ of events <m>E</m> and <m>F</m>, denoted _____, is the event that contains all outcomes in the sample space which are in <m>E</m> or <m>F</m>, or are in _____ <m>E</m> and <m>F</m>. 
                  </p>
                </li>
              </ol>
            </p>
          </statement>
          <answer>
            <p>
              <ol>
                <li>
                  <p>
                    A(n) random experiment is an activity or observation whose results cannot be predicted ahead of time.
                  </p>
                </li>
                <li>
                  <p>
                    Outcomes are the results of a random experiment.
                  </p>
                </li>
                <li>
                  <p>
                    The sample space, <m>S</m>, is the set of all possible outcomes for a random experiment.
                  </p>
                </li>
                <li>
                  <p>
                    An event, <m>E</m>, is a subset of the sample space (a collection of outcomes from an experiment).
                  </p>
                </li>
                <li>
                  <p>
                    A(n) simple event is an event with exactly one outcome.
                  </p>
                </li>
                <li>
                  <p>
                    The certain event, often denoted by <m>S</m>, is the event containing all outcomes of an experiment (the entire sample space).
                  </p>
                </li>
                <li>
                  <p>
                    A(n) impossible event, often denoted as <m>\{\}</m> or <m>\emptyset</m>, is an event containing no outcomes.
                  </p>
                </li>
                <li>
                  <p>
                    In general, for a sample space with <m>n</m> outcomes, there are <m>n</m> simple events, and the total number of possible events is <m>2^n</m>.
                  </p>
                </li>
                <li>
                  <p>
                    The complement of event <m>A</m>, denoted by <m>A^c</m>, is the event that contains all outcomes in the sample space which are NOT in event <m>A</m>.
                  </p>
                </li>
                <li>
                  <p>
                    The intersection of events <m>E</m> and <m>F</m>, denoted <m>E \cap F</m>, is the event that contains all outcomes in the sample space which both <m>E</m> and <m>F</m> have in common.
                  </p>
                </li>
                <li>
                  <p>
                    If two events, <m>E</m> and <m>F</m>, have no outcomes in common, then their intersection is impossible, <m>E \cap F = \emptyset</m>; the events <m>E</m> and <m>F</m> are called mutually exclusive, or disjoint, events.
                  </p>
                </li>
                <li>
                  <p>
                    The union of events <m>E</m> and <m>F</m>, denoted <m>E \cup F</m>, is the event that contains all outcomes in the sample space which are in <m>E</m> or <m>F</m>, or are in both <m>E</m> and <m>F</m>. 
                  </p>
                </li>
              </ol>
            </p>
          </answer>
      </exercise>
    </paragraphs>
    <paragraphs><!--   %%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%   SECTION 2  %%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%    -->
      <title>Let Us Get Our Hands On</title>
      <exercise number="1"><!-- #1 -->
        <introduction>
          <p>
            The graph of the linear programming problem
              <md>
                <mrow>\text{Maximize } \amp Z = 6x+4y</mrow>
                <mrow>\text{subject to } \amp 5x+y\leq 25</mrow>
                <mrow>\amp -x+2y\leq 6</mrow>
                <mrow>\amp 3x+5y\leq 26</mrow>
                <mrow>\amp x\geq 0, y\geq 0</mrow>
              </md>
            is given below.
            <image source="h5/images/h5fig1" width="80%">
              <shortdescription>Graph of the linear programming problem above. Refer to image description for more detail.</shortdescription><!--This is what a screenreader reads. Do not include math mode here.-->
              <description><!--this is not automatically read by a screenreader, but user can navigate there. Math mode is acceptable here and paragraph environment must be included-->
                <p>
                  A graph of the linear programming problem above, on coordinate axes with <m>x</m>-values from <m>-2</m> to <m>9</m> and <m>y</m>-values from <m>-3</m> to <m>7</m>.
                  The solution set is marked with an <m>S</m>. All lines are marked with their corresponding equations and all intersection points between those lines are marked with solid dots.
                </p>
              </description>
            </image>
          </p>
        </introduction>
        <task>
          <statement>
            <p>
              The initial simplex tableau for this linear programming problem is given below.
              <me>
                \begin{array}{rrrrrr|r}
                  x \amp y \amp s_1 \amp s_2 \amp s_3 \amp Z \amp c \\
                  \hline
                  5 \amp 1 \amp 1 \amp 0 \amp 0 \amp 0 \amp 25 \\
                  -1 \amp 2 \amp 0 \amp 1 \amp 0 \amp 0 \amp 6 \\
                  3 \amp 5 \amp 0 \amp 0 \amp 1 \amp 0 \amp 26 \\
                  \hline
                  -6 \amp -4 \amp 0 \amp 0 \amp 0 \amp 1 \amp 0
                \end{array}
              </me>
            Identify the basic and non-basic columns of the tableau, then state the solution at this stage of the simplex. Label this solution as "<m>A</m>" on the graph.
            </p>
          </statement>
          <answer>
            <p>
            Basic: <m>s_1, s_2, s_3, Z</m>;
            Non-basic: <m>x,y</m>;
            Solution: <m>x=0</m>, <m>y=0</m>, <m>s_1=25</m>, <m>s_2=6</m>, <m>s_3=26</m>, <m>Z=0</m>
            <image source="h5/images/h5fig2" width="80%">
              <shortdescription>Graph of the linear programming problem above with the first solution point marked. Refer to image description for more detail.</shortdescription><!--This is what a screenreader reads. Do not include math mode here.-->
              <description><!--this is not automatically read by a screenreader, but user can navigate there. Math mode is acceptable here and paragraph environment must be included-->
                <p>
                  A graph of the linear programming problem above, on coordinate axes with <m>x</m>-values from <m>-3</m> to <m>9</m> and <m>y</m>-values from <m>-3</m> to <m>7</m>.
                  The solution set is marked with an <m>S</m>. All lines are marked with their corresponding equations and all intersection points between those lines are marked with solid dots.
                  The dot at <m>(0,0)</m> is marked with an "<m>A</m>".
                </p>
              </description>
            </image>
            </p>
          </answer>
        </task>
        <task>
          <statement>
            <p>
              The pivot element of the tableau above is in column #<m>1</m>, row #<m>1</m> (be sure you know why). Performing the row operations on this element gives the tableau below.
              <me>
                \begin{array}{rrrrrr|r}
                  x \amp y \amp s_1 \amp s_2 \amp s_3 \amp Z \amp c \\
                  \hline
                  1 \amp \dfrac{1}{5} \amp \dfrac{1}{5} \amp 0 \amp 0 \amp 0 \amp 5 \\
                  0 \amp \dfrac{11}{5} \amp \dfrac{1}{5} \amp 1 \amp 0 \amp 0 \amp 11 \\
                  0 \amp \dfrac{22}{5} \amp -\dfrac{3}{5} \amp 0 \amp 1 \amp 0 \amp 11 \\
                  \hline
                  0 \amp -\dfrac{14}{5} \amp \dfrac{6}{5} \amp 0 \amp 0 \amp 1 \amp 30
                \end{array}
              </me>
              Identify the basic and non-basic columns of the tableau, then state the solution at this stage of the simplex. Label this solution as "<m>B</m>" on the graph.
            </p>
          </statement>
          <answer>
            <p>
            Basic: <m>x, s_2, s_3, Z</m>;
            Non-basic: <m>y,s_1</m>;
            Solution: <m>x=5</m>, <m>y=0</m>, <m>s_1=0</m>, <m>s_2=11</m>, <m>s_3=11</m>, <m>Z=30</m>
            <image source="h5/images/h5fig3" width="80%">
              <shortdescription>Graph of the linear programming problem above with the first and second solution points marked. Refer to image description for more detail.</shortdescription><!--This is what a screenreader reads. Do not include math mode here.-->
              <description><!--this is not automatically read by a screenreader, but user can navigate there. Math mode is acceptable here and paragraph environment must be included-->
                <p>
                  A graph of the linear programming problem above, on coordinate axes with <m>x</m>-values from <m>-3</m> to <m>9</m> and <m>y</m>-values from <m>-3</m> to <m>7</m>.
                  The solution set is marked with an <m>S</m>. All lines are marked with their corresponding equations and all intersection points between those lines are marked with solid dots.
                  The dot at <m>(0,0)</m> is marked with an "<m>A</m>".
                  The dot at <m>(5,0)</m> is marked with an "<m>B</m>".
                </p>
              </description>
            </image>
            </p>
          </answer>
        </task>
        <task>
          <statement>
            <p>
              The pivot element of the tableau above is in column #<m>2</m>, row #<m>3</m> (be sure you know why). Performing the row operations on this element gives the tableau below.
              <me>
                \begin{array}{rrrrrr|r}
                  x \amp y \amp s_1 \amp s_2 \amp s_3 \amp Z \amp c \\
                  \hline
                  1 \amp 0 \amp \dfrac{5}{22} \amp 0 \amp -\dfrac{1}{22} \amp 0 \amp \dfrac{9}{2} \\
                  0 \amp 0 \amp \dfrac{1}{2} \amp 1 \amp -\dfrac{1}{2} \amp 0 \amp \dfrac{11}{2} \\
                  0 \amp 1 \amp -\dfrac{3}{22} \amp 0 \amp \dfrac{5}{22} \amp 0 \amp \dfrac{5}{2} \\
                  \hline
                  0 \amp 0 \amp \dfrac{9}{11} \amp 0 \amp \dfrac{7}{11} \amp 1 \amp 37
                \end{array}
              </me>
              Identify the basic and non-basic columns of the tableau, then state the solution at this stage of the simplex. Label this solution as "<m>C</m>" on the graph.
            </p>
          </statement>
          <answer>
            <p>
            Basic: <m>x, y, s_2, Z</m>;
            Non-basic: <m>s_1, s_3</m>;
            Solution: <m>x=\dfrac{9}{2}</m>, <m>y=\dfrac{5}{2}</m>, <m>s_1=0</m>, <m>s_2=\dfrac{11}{2}</m>, <m>s_3=0</m>, <m>Z=37</m>
            <image source="h5/images/h5fig4" width="80%">
              <shortdescription>Graph of the linear programming problem above with the first, second, and third solution points marked. Refer to image description for more detail.</shortdescription><!--This is what a screenreader reads. Do not include math mode here.-->
              <description><!--this is not automatically read by a screenreader, but user can navigate there. Math mode is acceptable here and paragraph environment must be included-->
                <p>
                  A graph of the linear programming problem above, on coordinate axes with <m>x</m>-values from <m>-3</m> to <m>9</m> and <m>y</m>-values from <m>-3</m> to <m>7</m>.
                  The solution set is marked with an <m>S</m>. All lines are marked with their corresponding equations and all intersection points between those lines are marked with solid dots.
                  The dot at <m>(0,0)</m> is marked with an "<m>A</m>".
                  The dot at <m>(5,0)</m> is marked with an "<m>B</m>".
                  The dot at <m>(4.5,2.5)</m> is marked with an "<m>C</m>".
                </p>
              </description>
            </image>
            </p>
          </answer>
        </task>
        <task>
          <statement>
            <p>
              The last solution, <m>Z=37</m>, is the optimal solution. How do you know this?
            </p>
          </statement>
          <answer>
            <p>
              There are no negative entries in the bottom row.
            </p>
          </answer>
        </task>
      </exercise>
      <exercise number="2"><!-- #2 -->
        <introduction>
          <p>
            For each simplex tableau, determine whether it is in its final form. If it is, find the optimal solution. If it is not, state the next pivot entry.
          </p>
        </introduction>
        <task>
          <statement>
            <p>
              <me>
                \begin{array}{rrrrrrr|r}
                  x \amp y \amp s_1 \amp s_2 \amp s_3 \amp s_4 \amp P \amp c \\
                  \hline
                  2 \amp 3 \amp 1 \amp 0 \amp 0 \amp 0 \amp 0 \amp 6 \\
                  -3 \amp -2 \amp 0 \amp 1 \amp 0 \amp 0 \amp 0 \amp 3 \\
                  0 \amp 2 \amp 0 \amp 0 \amp 1 \amp 0 \amp 0 \amp 5 \\
                  2 \amp 1 \amp 0 \amp 0 \amp 0 \amp 1 \amp 0 \amp 4 \\
                  \hline
                  -4 \amp -3 \amp 0 \amp 0 \amp 0 \amp 0 \amp 1 \amp 0
                \end{array}
              </me>
            </p>
          </statement>
          <answer>
            <p>
              The next pivot element is <m>2</m> in column <m>1</m>, row <m>4</m>.
            </p>
          </answer>
        </task>
        <task>
          <statement>
            <p>
              <me>
                \begin{array}{rrrrrr|r}
                  x \amp y \amp s_1 \amp s_2 \amp s_3 \amp P \amp c \\
                  \hline
                  0 \amp 0 \amp 1 \amp 0 \amp -8 \amp 0 \amp 56 \\
                  -\dfrac{7}{2} \amp 0 \amp 0 \amp 1 \amp -2 \amp 0 \amp 24 \\
                  \dfrac{1}{4} \amp 1 \amp 0 \amp 0 \amp 1 \amp 0 \amp 0 \\
                  \hline
                  -13 \amp 0 \amp 0 \amp 0 \amp 12 \amp 1 \amp 0
                \end{array}
              </me>
            </p>
          </statement>
          <answer>
            <p>
              The next pivot element is <m>\dfrac{1}{4}</m> in column <m>1</m>, row <m>3</m>.
            </p>
          </answer>
        </task>
        <task>
          <statement>
            <p>
              <me>
                \begin{array}{rrrrr|r}
                  x \amp y \amp s_1 \amp s_2 \amp P \amp c \\
                  \hline
                  1 \amp 0 \amp \dfrac{3}{4} \amp -\dfrac{1}{4} \amp 0 \amp 3 \\
                  0 \amp 1 \amp -\dfrac{1}{2} \amp \dfrac{1}{2} \amp 0 \amp 4 \\
                  \hline
                  0 \amp 0 \amp \dfrac{3}{2} \amp \dfrac{1}{2} \amp 1 \amp 24
                \end{array}
              </me>
            </p>
          </statement>
          <answer>
            <p>
              The optimal solution is <m>P=24</m> when <m>x=3</m> and <m>y=4</m>.
            </p>
          </answer>
        </task>
        <task>
          <statement>
            <p>
              <me>
                \begin{array}{rrrrrrr|r}
                  x \amp y \amp z \amp s_1 \amp s_2 \amp s_3 \amp P \amp c \\
                  \hline
                  0 \amp 0 \amp 1 \amp 0 \amp -8 \amp \dfrac{1}{2} \amp 0 \amp 56 \\
                  0 \amp 14 \amp 0 \amp 1 \amp 12 \amp 0 \amp 0 \amp 24 \\
                  1 \amp 4 \amp 0 \amp 0 \amp 4 \amp -1 \amp 0 \amp 48 \\
                  \hline
                  0 \amp 52 \amp 0 \amp 0 \amp 64 \amp \dfrac{2}{3} \amp 1 \amp 240
                \end{array}
              </me>
            </p>
          </statement>
          <answer>
            <p>
              The optimal solution is <m>P=240</m> when <m>x=48</m>, <m>y=0</m>, and <m>z=56</m>.
            </p>
          </answer>
        </task>
      </exercise>
      <exercise number="3"><!-- #3 -->
        <introduction>
          <p>
            A company builds educational robot kits designed for children. They produce three types of robot kits: beginner, explorer, and research.
            Each beginner kit requires <m>1</m> motor, <m>2</m> sensors, and <m>1</m> battery pack.
            Each explorer kit requires <m>2</m> motors, <m>2</m> sensors, and <m>2</m> battery packs.
            Each research kit requires <m>6</m> motors, <m>4</m> sensors, and <m>2</m> battery packs.
            The profit earned for each beginner, explorer, and research kit is <m>\$25</m>, <m>\$65</m>, and <m>\$80</m> respectively.
            The company has <m>500</m> motors, <m>700</m> sensors, and <m>300</m> battery packs in inventory.
            How many of each type of robot kit should be produced to maximize profit?
          </p>
        </introduction>
        <task>
          <statement>
            <p>
              Set up the linear programming problem (i.e., constraints and objective function) that represents this scenario.
            </p>
          </statement>
          <answer>  
            <p>
              <md>
                <mrow>b \amp =\text{\# of beginner kits produced}</mrow>
                <mrow>e \amp =\text{\# of explorer kits produced}</mrow>
                <mrow>r \amp =\text{\# of research kits produced}</mrow>
                <mrow>P \amp =\text{profit (in dollars) from selling produced robot kits}</mrow>
              </md>
              <md>
                <mrow>\text{Maximize } \amp P=25b+65e+80r</mrow>
                <mrow>\text{subject to } \amp b+2e+6r\leq 500</mrow>
                <mrow>\amp 2b+2e+4r\leq 700</mrow>
                <mrow>\amp b+2e+2r\leq 300</mrow>
                <mrow>\amp b,e,r \geq 0</mrow>
              </md>
            </p>
          </answer>
        </task>
        <task>
          <statement>
            <p>
              Give the initial simplex tableau that represents this linear programming problem.
            </p>
          </statement>
          <answer>
            <p>
              <me>
                \begin{array}{rrrrrrr|r}
                  b \amp e \amp r \amp s_1 \amp s_2 \amp s_3 \amp P \amp c \\
                  \hline
                  1 \amp 2 \amp 6 \amp 1 \amp 0 \amp 0 \amp 0 \amp 500 \\
                  2 \amp 2 \amp 4 \amp 0 \amp 1 \amp 0 \amp 0 \amp 700 \\
                  1 \amp 2 \amp 2 \amp 0 \amp 0 \amp 1 \amp 0 \amp 300 \\
                  \hline
                  -25 \amp -65 \amp -80 \amp 0 \amp 0 \amp 0 \amp 1 \amp 0
                \end{array}
              </me>
            </p>
          </answer>
        </task>
        <task>
          <statement>
            <p>
              The final simplex tableau is given below where <m>b</m> is the number of beginner kits produced, <m>e</m> is the 
              number of explorer kits produced, <m>r</m> is the number of research kits produced, <m>s_1</m> is the slack variable for
              the number of motors left over, <m>s_2</m> is the slack variable for the number of sensors left over, <m>s_3</m> is the 
              slack variable for the number of battery packs left over, and <m>P</m> is the profit earned, in dollars.
              <me>
                \begin{array}{rrrrrrr|r}
                  b \amp e \amp r \amp s_1 \amp s_2 \amp s_3 \amp P \amp c \\
                  \hline
                  0 \amp 0 \amp 1 \amp \dfrac{1}{4} \amp 0 \amp -\dfrac{1}{4} \amp 0 \amp 50 \\
                  1 \amp 0 \amp 0 \amp -\dfrac{1}{2} \amp 1 \amp -\dfrac{1}{2} \amp 0 \amp 300 \\
                  \dfrac{1}{2} \amp 1 \amp 0 \amp -\dfrac{1}{4} \amp 0 \amp \dfrac{3}{4} \amp 0 \amp 100 \\
                  \hline
                  \dfrac{15}{2} \amp 0 \amp 0 \amp \dfrac{15}{4} \amp 0 \amp \dfrac{115}{4} \amp 1 \amp 10500
                \end{array}
              </me>
              Give the optimal solution for this problem.
            </p>
          </statement>
          <answer>
            <p>
              <m>P=10500</m> when <m>b=0</m>, <m>e=100</m>, and <m>r=50</m>.
            </p>
          </answer>
        </task>
      </exercise>
      <exercise number="4"><!-- #4 -->
        <introduction>
          <p>
            Consider a two-stage experiment consisting of rolling a standard fair four-sided die and noting whether the number shown is odd or even, then drawing a distinct letter from the word 'GIGEM'.
          </p>
        </introduction>
        <task>
          <statement>
            <p>
              State the sample space.
            </p>
          </statement>
          <answer>
            <p>
              <m>S=\{(\text{odd},\text{G}),(\text{odd},\text{I}),(\text{odd},\text{E}),(\text{odd},\text{M}),(\text{even},\text{G}),(\text{even},\text{I}),(\text{even},\text{E}),(\text{even},\text{M})\}</m>
            </p>
          </answer>
        </task>
        <task>
          <statement>
            <p>
              How many total possible events are there in this experiment?
            </p>
          </statement>
          <answer>
            <p>
              <m>2^8=256</m>
            </p>
          </answer>
        </task>
      </exercise>
        <exercise number="5"><!-- #5 -->
        <introduction>
          <p>
            A student draws a card from a standard deck of <m>52</m> cards, noting the suit, then flips a coin, noting which side of the coin (heads or tails) lands facing up.
          </p>
        </introduction>
        <task>
          <statement>
            <p>
              Make a tree diagram that illustrates the sample space of this experiment.
            </p>
          </statement>
          <answer>
            <p>
            <image source="h5/images/h5fig5" width="80%">
              <shortdescription>A tree diagram for this problem. Refer to image description for more detail.</shortdescription><!--This is what a screenreader reads. Do not include math mode here.-->
              <description><!--this is not automatically read by a screenreader, but user can navigate there. Math mode is acceptable here and paragraph environment must be included-->
                <p>
                  A tree diagram for this problem. The words "heart", "spade", "diamond", and "club" come out of a solid dot. The letters "H" and "T" come out of each of the four words.
                </p>
              </description>
            </image>
            </p>
          </answer>
        </task>
        <task>
          <statement>
            <p>
              How many total possible events are there in this experiment?
            </p>
          </statement>
          <answer>
            <p>
              <m>2^8=256</m>
            </p>
          </answer>
        </task>
        </exercise>
        <exercise number="6"><!-- #6 -->
        <introduction>
          <p>
            An experiment consists of drawing a card from a standard deck of <m>52</m> cards, noting the color, and then rolling a six-sided die, noting the number rolled.
            Let <m>E</m> be the event "a black card is drawn", <m>F</m> be the event "a number less than <m>4</m> is rolled", and <m>G</m> be the event "a multiple of <m>3</m> is rolled".
            Describe and list the outcomes in the events below.
          </p>
        </introduction>
        <task>
          <statement>
            <p>
              Give the sample space for the experiment.
            </p>
          </statement>
          <answer>
            <p>
              <m>S=\{(R,1), (R,2), (R,3), (R,4), (R,5), (R,6), (B,1), (B,2), (B,3), (B,4), (B,5), (B,6)\}</m>
            </p>
          </answer>
        </task>
        <task>
          <statement>
            <p>
              Describe and list the outcomes in the event <m>E^c \cup F</m>.
            </p>
          </statement>
          <answer>
            <p>
              <m>E^c\cup F = \{(R,1),(R,2),(R,3),(R,4),(R,5),(R,6),(B,1),(B,2),(B,3)\}</m> is the event a red card is drawn or a number less than <m>4</m> is rolled.
            </p>
          </answer>
        </task>
        <task>
          <statement>
            <p>
              Describe and list the outcomes in the event <m>(G\cap F)^c</m>.
            </p>
          </statement>
          <answer>
            <p>
              <m>(G\cap F)^c=\{(R,1),(R,2),(R,4),(R,5),(R,6),(B,1),(B,2),(B,4),(B,5),(B,6)\}</m> is the event that a non-multiple of <m>3</m> is rolled or a number greater than or equal to <m>4</m> is rolled.
            </p>
          </answer>
        </task>
        <task>
          <statement>
            <p>
              Describe and list the outcomes in <m>(E \cup F) \cap G</m>.
            </p>
          </statement>
          <answer>
            <p>
              <m>(E \cup F) \cap G = \{(R,3),(B,3),(B,6)\}</m> is the event a black card is drawn or a number less than <m>4</m> is rolled and a multiple of <m>3</m> is rolled.
            </p>
          </answer>
        </task>
        <task>
          <statement>
            <p>
              Write the symbolic notation for the event "a red card is drawn or a number less than <m>4</m> is rolled, but not a multiple of <m>3</m>".
            </p>
          </statement>
          <answer>
            <p>
              <m>(E^c\cup F)\cap G^c</m>
            </p>
          </answer>
        </task>
        </exercise>
        <exercise number="7"><!-- #7 -->
        <introduction>
          <p>
            Given sample space <m>S</m> and two events <m>A</m> and <m>B</m>, shade in the given set in each Venn diagram.
          </p>
        </introduction>
        <task>
          <statement>
            <p>
              <m>A \cup B^c</m>
            <image source="h5/images/h5fig6" width="40%">
              <shortdescription>An empty Venn diagram. Refer to image description for more detail.</shortdescription><!--This is what a screenreader reads. Do not include math mode here.-->
              <description><!--this is not automatically read by a screenreader, but user can navigate there. Math mode is acceptable here and paragraph environment must be included-->
                <p>
                  An empty Venn diagram. The circles overlap.
                </p>
              </description>
            </image>
            </p>
          </statement>
          <answer>
            <p>
            <image source="h5/images/h5fig7" width="40%">
              <shortdescription>A Venn diagram for this problem. Refer to image description for more detail.</shortdescription><!--This is what a screenreader reads. Do not include math mode here.-->
              <description><!--this is not automatically read by a screenreader, but user can navigate there. Math mode is acceptable here and paragraph environment must be included-->
                <p>
                  A Venn diagram for this problem. The circles overlap. The region in circle <m>A</m>, as well as the region not contained within either circle, are shaded.
                </p>
              </description>
            </image>
            </p>
          </answer>
        </task>
        <task>
          <statement>
            <p>
              <m>A^c \cap B^c</m>
            <image source="h5/images/h5fig8" width="40%">
              <shortdescription>An empty Venn diagram. Refer to image description for more detail.</shortdescription><!--This is what a screenreader reads. Do not include math mode here.-->
              <description><!--this is not automatically read by a screenreader, but user can navigate there. Math mode is acceptable here and paragraph environment must be included-->
                <p>
                  An empty Venn diagram. The circles overlap.
                </p>
              </description>
            </image>
            </p>
          </statement>
          <answer>
            <p>
            <image source="h5/images/h5fig9" width="40%">
              <shortdescription>A Venn diagram for this problem. Refer to image description for more detail.</shortdescription><!--This is what a screenreader reads. Do not include math mode here.-->
              <description><!--this is not automatically read by a screenreader, but user can navigate there. Math mode is acceptable here and paragraph environment must be included-->
                <p>
                  A Venn diagram for this problem. The circles overlap. The region not contained within either circle is shaded.
                </p>
              </description>
            </image>
            </p>
          </answer>
        </task>
        </exercise>
        <exercise number="8"><!-- #8 -->
          <statement>
            <p>
              Given sample space <m>S</m> and two mutually exclusive events <m>A</m> and <m>B</m>, draw a Venn diagram that represents <m>B\cup A^c</m>.
            </p>
          </statement>
          <answer>
            <p>
            <image source="h5/images/h5fig10" width="40%">
              <shortdescription>A Venn diagram for this problem. Refer to image description for more detail.</shortdescription><!--This is what a screenreader reads. Do not include math mode here.-->
              <description><!--this is not automatically read by a screenreader, but user can navigate there. Math mode is acceptable here and paragraph environment must be included-->
                <p>
                  A Venn diagram for this problem. The circles do not overlap. The region outside of circle <m>A</m> is shaded.
                </p>
              </description>
            </image>
            </p>
          </answer>
        </exercise>
    </paragraphs>

    <paragraphs><!--   %%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%   SECTION 3  %%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%    -->
      <title>Now Grades Go Up</title>
      
      <exercise number="1"><!-- #1 -->
        <statement>
          <p>
            Choose the statement below that best describes whether the optimal solution for the given simplex tableau has been reached.
              <me>
                \begin{array}{rrrrrrr|r}
                  x \amp y \amp z \amp s_1 \amp s_2 \amp s_3 \amp P \amp c \\
                  \hline
                  -2 \amp 1 \amp \dfrac{3}{4} \amp \dfrac{1}{2} \amp 0 \amp 0 \amp 0 \amp 5000 \\
                  0 \amp 0 \amp \dfrac{1}{2} \amp -\dfrac{1}{3} \amp 1 \amp 0 \amp 0 \amp 1250 \\
                  \dfrac{1}{3} \amp 0 \amp \dfrac{1}{4} \amp -\dfrac{1}{6} \amp 0 \amp 1 \amp 0 \amp 300 \\
                  \hline
                  -3 \amp 0 \amp -2 \amp 8 \amp 0 \amp 0 \amp 1 \amp 45625
                \end{array}
              </me>
          </p>
            <ol marker="(A)">
              <li>
                <p>
                  The optimal solution has been reached because no further pivots are necessary, and the optimal solution is <m>P=45625</m> when <m>(x,y,z)=(0,1250,0)</m>.
                </p>
              </li>
              <li>
                <p>
                  The optimal solution has not been reached, and the next pivot element is the <m>\dfrac{3}{4}</m> in row <m>1</m>, column <m>3</m>.
                </p>
              </li>
              <li>
                <p>
                  The optimal solution has been reached because no further pivots are necessary and the optimal solution is <m>P=45625</m> when <m>(x,y,z)=(5000,1250,300)</m>.
                </p>
              </li>
              <li>
                <p>
                  The optimal solution has not been reached, and the next pivot element is the <m>-2</m> in row <m>1</m>, column <m>1</m>.
                </p>
              </li>
              <li>
                <p>
                  The optimal solution has not been reached, and the next pivot element is the <m>\dfrac{1}{3}</m> in row <m>3</m>, column <m>1</m>.
                </p>
              </li>
            </ol>
        </statement>
        <answer>
          <p>
            (E)
          </p>
        </answer>
      </exercise>

      <exercise number="2"><!-- #2 -->
        <statement>
          <p>
            In the Venn diagrams below, which one has a grey shaded area that represents <m>A^c\cup B</m>?
          </p>
            <ol marker="(A)">
              <li>
                <p>
            <image source="h5/images/h5fig11" width="40%">
              <shortdescription>A Venn diagram for this problem. Refer to image description for more detail.</shortdescription><!--This is what a screenreader reads. Do not include math mode here.-->
              <description><!--this is not automatically read by a screenreader, but user can navigate there. Math mode is acceptable here and paragraph environment must be included-->
                <p>
                  A Venn diagram for this problem. The circles overlap. The region contained in circle <m>B</m> and the region not contained within either circle are shaded.
                </p>
              </description>
            </image>
                </p>
              </li>
              <li>
                <p>
              <image source="h5/images/h5fig12" width="40%">
              <shortdescription>A Venn diagram for this problem. Refer to image description for more detail.</shortdescription><!--This is what a screenreader reads. Do not include math mode here.-->
              <description><!--this is not automatically read by a screenreader, but user can navigate there. Math mode is acceptable here and paragraph environment must be included-->
                <p>
                  A Venn diagram for this problem. The circles overlap. The region not contained within circle <m>A</m> is shaded.
                </p>
              </description>
            </image>
                </p>
              </li>
              <li>
                <p>
            <image source="h5/images/h5fig13" width="40%">
              <shortdescription>A Venn diagram for this problem. Refer to image description for more detail.</shortdescription><!--This is what a screenreader reads. Do not include math mode here.-->
              <description><!--this is not automatically read by a screenreader, but user can navigate there. Math mode is acceptable here and paragraph environment must be included-->
                <p>
                  A Venn diagram for this problem. The circles overlap. The region only contained within circle <m>B</m> but not <m>A</m> is shaded.
                </p>
              </description>
            </image>
                </p>
              </li>
              <li>
                <p>
            <image source="h5/images/h5fig14" width="40%">
              <shortdescription>A Venn diagram for this problem. Refer to image description for more detail.</shortdescription><!--This is what a screenreader reads. Do not include math mode here.-->
              <description><!--this is not automatically read by a screenreader, but user can navigate there. Math mode is acceptable here and paragraph environment must be included-->
                <p>
                  A Venn diagram for this problem. The circles overlap. The region not contained within either circle is shaded.
                </p>
              </description>
            </image>
                </p>
              </li>
              <li>
                <p>
            <image source="h5/images/h5fig15" width="40%">
              <shortdescription>A Venn diagram for this problem. Refer to image description for more detail.</shortdescription><!--This is what a screenreader reads. Do not include math mode here.-->
              <description><!--this is not automatically read by a screenreader, but user can navigate there. Math mode is acceptable here and paragraph environment must be included-->
